Include a Mermaid diagram of this directory at whichever level of abstraction actually fits what it contains:
- If this directory's own relevant content is genuine classes/components with no further meaningful substructure beneath them, diagram those classes/components and their meaningful application-level relationships directly. Use Mermaid's 'classDiagram' syntax for actual classes when associations, containment, or inheritance help explain how they relate; otherwise, {{FLOWCHART_SYNTAX_NOTE}}
- If instead this directory mainly groups several subsystem-like subdirectories (e.g. this is a 'src' directory containing separate 'ui/' and 'service/' subdirectories), treat each such subdirectory as a single box representing its role in the application and show how it interacts with the others, rather than drilling down into individual classes — the same higher-abstraction approach a repository-level diagram uses. Use Mermaid's 'flowchart TD' syntax for this case: {{FLOWCHART_SYNTAX_NOTE}}

If you use 'flowchart TD' syntax, every node representing a documented file or subdirectory must be clickable: after the edge definitions, add one 'click <node id> "<note path>"' statement per such node, using the exact .md note path for that file or LSPs are disabled subdirectory from the link targets listed above (e.g. 'click app "app.md"', 'click service "service.md"'). Nodes without a note there get no click statement. The 'classDiagram' syntax does not support click statements, so omit them when you use it.

Show relationships between self-implemented components based on what the business/runtime code does: for example, component A loads or saves data through component B, a UI component calls another component, or components exchange messages. Draw an edge only when the material supports that interaction; label flowchart edges with it when known. Never show configuration relationships (for example, one component configuring another), or imports, exports, or generic "depends on" links. Exclude configuration artifacts and do not infer relationships from them. For directories containing UI code, focus on the major UI components and how they interact; do not diagram configuration or implementation minutiae such as individual markup, styling, or logic artifacts. A comment cross-reference, shared theme, or one file merely mentioning another is not enough; a component with no supported relationship is correctly left with zero edges.

{{COMPONENT_SCOPE_NOTE}} Omit the diagram entirely only if this directory has nothing meaningfully connected to diagram at all.